<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">East Francia</h1><p class="st-meta">843 CE – 962 CE · capital Regensburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The eastern Carolingian kingdom created at Verdun in 843, from which the Kingdom of Germany and the Holy Roman Empire developed.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in 843 divided the Carolingian empire among the three sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> received the lands east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-worms">Worms</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-speyer">Speyer</a> on its west bank. He ruled them from palaces such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-regensburg">Regensburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, and in 870 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-meerssen">Treaty of Meerssen</a> added much of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-lotharingia">Lotharingia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>East Francia combined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/franks">Franks</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saxons">Saxons</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a>, Swabians and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-the-thuringians">Thuringians</a>, each with strong regional identities that grew into stem duchies. Kings moved between palaces and relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and great abbeys. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnulf-of-carinthia">Arnulf of Carinthia</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dyle">Dyle</a> in 891 and was crowned emperor in 896, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> raids devastated the kingdom after 900.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the last Carolingian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a>, died in 911, the nobles elected Conrad I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, then in 919 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler of Saxony</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry's</a> son Otto I defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Lechfeld</a> in 955 and was crowned emperor in 962, a date usually taken as the start of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-germany">Kingdom of Germany</a> within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/east-francia.jpg" alt="East Francia" width="320" height="243"><figcaption>Image: Blank map of Europe.svg: maix¿? derivative work: Alphathon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_Francia_843.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>843 CE – 962 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Regensburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Louis the German, Arnulf of Carinthia, Henry the Fowler</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old High German, Old Saxon, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>, <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of the East Franks, Francia Orientalis</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=902&amp;focus=east-francia" aria-label="Eas